          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (or more generally
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (meaning a
            = succeed ")," do
              %em 1-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-level"} h-level
            restriction on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %strong precategory
            is a category in which
            %em no
            such restriction is imposed; thus the type of objects may be a set (i.e. a 0-type), or a 1-type, or an even higher type.
          %p
            Surprisingly much
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            can be done purely with precategories, and some authors call them merely “categories”. However, there are notable differences between precategories and the familiar notion of “category” from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set-theoretic
            foundations. Moreover, the two do not coincide under the standard interpretation of homotopy type theory into set theory (e.g. the model in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+space"} Segal space
          %p
            Alternative notions of “1-category” in homotopy type theory include
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+categories"} strict categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+categories"} univalent categories
          %p
            There are also “even more pre-” notions of category in intensional type theory, such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-categories"} E-categories
