                  %mo ×
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} hom
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isPrecat}(A) \coloneqq \mathrm{isSegal}(A) \times \prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \mathrm{isSet}(\mathrm{hom}_A(x, y))
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The interpretation of the notion of precategory in the model of homotopy type theory in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
                yields a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+space"} Segal space
                in which the morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_1 \to A_0 \times A_0
                has homotopically discrete fibers.
            %li
              %p
                The definition of precategory is evidently a type-theoretic notion of the definition of category with
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category#AFamilyOfCollectionsOfMorphisms"} a family of collections of morphisms
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category#OneCollectionOfMorphisms"} one collection of morphisms
                could be given, but it would be much less convenient to use. In particular, the “type of all morphisms”
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                          %mo :
                          %mi Ob
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{A,B:Ob(\mathcal{C})} Hom(A,B)
                in a precategory is
                %em not
                itself a set, since it can incorporate nontrivial identifications from
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ob
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(\mathcal{C})
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_1 \to \mathcal{C}_0
                is 0-truncated” (i.e. its fibers are sets).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Every
            %strong precategory
            is a locally univalent
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-category"} E-category
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+type+theory"} univalent type theory
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+identity+principle"} structure identity principle
            for precategories:
          %p
            Equality of precategories in a univalent universe is the same as having a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful"} fully faithful
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalent-on-objects+functor"} equivalent-on-objects functor
            between precategories in the univalent universe.
          %p This is because fully faithful functors establish an equivalence of hom-sets between the two precategories, and equivalent-on-objects functors establish an equivalence of object types between the two precategories, which means that fully faithful equivalent-on-objects functors is the right notion of equivalence between precategories.
          %p
            Only in univalent categories are such functors the same as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
